bed as being on the sides of the  truck and kevin goff the defense  attorney for brian got out that brian  was distressed and unfocused during the  investigation during the lunch break  civil rights attorney lee merritt was  joined by benjamin crump and reverend al  sharpton discussing the makeup of the  jury along with how it broke their heart  to see officers check on the mcmichaels  as aubry's body lay their dying  all right obviously  we see there's some press conferences  there today with reverend al sharpton  with ben crump what were the the big  takeaways  the big takeaways were what the family  was reacting to as well as al sharpton  when he first heard about this case that  both the mcmichaels  were around  the scene of the crime and people seem  to be tending more to them than looking  after ahmad arbury  benjamin crump told us that he along  with lee mayor are filing a civil rights  claim against the county of glenn and  their police officers and the way in  which they handled this case at the  initial the exception of the the death  of a mod armory as well as attorney  jackie johnson who's facing her own  indictment charges yeah you know now  back inside the courtroom you you  mentioned there was evidence of these  smudges that were in william roddy  bryant's car and we hear arbor's family  had a big reaction to that what did ben  trump tell you  yeah so i actually got to speak with  benjamin crump one on one and he was a  little disgusted and said the family was  as well that when the defense attorney  was trying to make the distinctions that  these smudges or handprints were not on  the front or back of william bryan's  vehicle but merely on the side that that  suggested that he did not cut off a mod  arbory or did not try to run him over  with the vehicle trying to kind of  soften the blow so to speak that ahmad  abre was merely just i don't know  glancing by the vehicle or wasn't seen  in close proximity to the truck benjamin  crump along with the family uh kind of  took that in a sour no and didn't really  like how the defense was characterizing  what they said was very obvious from the  video yeah sometimes uh those efforts  pay off sometimes they backfire on you  hey brian thank you so much for the live  report from georgia we appreciate it hey  when we come back we're going to hear  from the turpin family survivors for the  first time since their rescue two brave  women speak out in an exclusive  interview detailing the abuse they  endured all at the hands of their  parents that's next on law and crime  daily  [Music]  and welcome back to law and crime daily  we're now hearing for the first time  from members of the turpin family  siblings who were subjected to abuse and  torture at the hands of their own  parents back in 2018 13 siblings were  rescued from their california home after  one sister escaped and made a 911  emergency call the 17 year old told  dispatchers one of her sisters was  chained to a bed and that she had not  been bathed in more than a year she also  said her parents claimed the children  attended a private school but at 17  years old she had completed only the  first grade at the time of their rescue  the siblings had been planning for an  escape for more than two years  in 2019 parents david and louise turpin  pled guilty to multiple charges  including torture and false imprisonment  they were both sentenced to life in  prison for the first time two of the  sisters tell their story in an exclusive  abc interview  my whole body was shaking i couldn't  really dial now on  because  i'm sorry i don't know how you had the  courage i think it was like us coming so  close to death so many times people were  chained for  what months  yeah  months  yeah unbearable  yeah  mother she choked me  and i thought i was going to die the  only word i know to call it is hell  how are these folks even surviving at  all julie the turpins were sentenced to  25 years to life  what are the chances they're ever going  to get parole  so i believe they're up for parole after  25 years um the question is after 25  years what's going to happen i mean you  know we think of murder cases and we  wonder is is this equal to that i'd say  it is these are kids that were  absolutely tortured for the most  important times of their lives they  didn't they were literally stuck in this  as she described hell um and so i think  it's hard-pressed for anybody anybody  including those children by the way who  are going to have a say as to whether or  not these parents ever get out are ever  ever ever going to think it's  appropriate to let them out of jail yeah  you know terry the turpins argued that  hey they love their children and  ultimately they pled guilty to the 14  counts of torture cruelty and false  imprisonment so how are you squaring  those two things  you know it's really hard to reconcile  those two things torture in california  is defined as intentionally inflicting  great bodily harm causing extreme pain  and cruelty and false imprisonment are  also intentional crimes so it's really  difficult to understand how you can  commit these intentional crimes and at  the same time  you are saying you love your children  either they are great liars because  those two things can't both be true or  in fact perhaps there was some mental  illness involved  we don't know whether or not that will  be a factor as far as getting out for  parole but frankly i don't see how you  can reconcile those two statements  because they are just complete opposites  of each other and frankly in my opinion  they do deserve to be in jail for the  rest of their life yeah very very  difficult situation so thank you both  julie rendellman criminal defense  attorney and co-host terry austin thank  you both thank you for joining us here  on law and crime daily we will see you  next time as we discuss justice in  america  [Music]  [Music]  you
 

